.TH CDE-RPC 1M "2026-11-19" "Amiga UNIX add-on" "CDE"
.SH NAME
cde-rpc \- start and stop CDE's RPC servers
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sh /etc/init.d/cde-rpc
.BR start " | " stop
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cde-rpc
starts CDE's two RPC servers at boot, each only when
.I /etc/rc.conf
turns it on.
Both are off as installed: nothing of CDE listens on the network until
the administrator chooses it.
.TP
.B ttdbserver=yes
starts
.BR rpc.ttdbserver ,
ToolTalk's database server (RPC program 100083).
ToolTalk uses it for objects and file-scoped messages shared between
machines; a desktop on one machine works without it.
.TP
.B cmsd=yes
starts
.BR rpc.cmsd ,
the calendar server (RPC program 100068).
The calendar,
.BR dtcm ,
opens every calendar through it, including the user's own.
.TP
.BI cde_rpc_wait= seconds
how long the boot waits for
.B rpcbind
before giving up (default 120).
.PP
Both servers run standalone and register themselves with
.BR rpcbind ,
which the stock
.I /etc/rc2.d/S75rpc
starts in the background.
So
.B start
hands the work to a background run (rc.subr's
.BR rc_bg ):
it waits until
.B rpcinfo \-p
answers, then starts each server that is turned on and not already
running, and the boot goes on.
.B stop
ends both.
The script is linked as
.I /etc/rc2.d/S78cde-rpc
and as
.I K38cde-rpc
in
.I /etc/rc0.d
and
.IR /etc/rc1.d .
Its messages are in
.IR /var/adm/rc.log ,
the background run's output in
.IR /var/adm/rclog/cde-rpc .
.SH EXAMPLES
Turn the calendar server on, now and at every boot:
.PP
.RS
.nf
echo cmsd=yes >> /etc/rc.conf
sh /etc/init.d/cde-rpc start
.fi
.RE
.SH DTSPCD
CDE's subprocess control daemon,
.BR dtspcd ,
lets another machine's desktop run actions on this one (TCP port 6112).
It is started by
.BR inetd ,
and no line for it is installed: it is needed only for remote
execution.
To turn it on, add the service and the inetd line by hand, then
restart inetd:
.PP
.RS
.nf
echo 'dtspc 6112/tcp' >> /etc/inet/services
echo 'dtspc stream tcp nowait root /usr/dt/bin/dtspcd /usr/dt/bin/dtspcd' >> /etc/inet/inetd.conf
sacadm -k -p inetd; sacadm -s -p inetd
.fi
.RE
.PP
To turn it off, remove the two lines and restart inetd the same way.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/rc.conf
the switches (rc.conf(4))
.TP
.I /usr/dt/bin/rpc.ttdbserver
.TP
.I /usr/dt/bin/rpc.cmsd
.SH SEE ALSO
rc.subr(1M), rc.conf(4), rpcbind(1M), rpcinfo(1M), inetd(1M), sacadm(1M)
